@page "/badge"

<CatHead Title="Badges">
    <code>.badge</code> is a neutral pill. The semantic modifiers carry the same meaning as the
    button families; the three categorical hues carry none at all.
</CatHead>

<CatGroup Title="Tones">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Badge.ToneMatrix" Title="Tone and emphasis" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            Every badge tone — the semantic ones, <code>.badge--brand</code> among them, and the three
            categorical hues — comes soft, <code>.badge--solid</code> or <code>.badge--ghost</code>, an outline.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Badge.Semantic" Title="Semantic tones">
        <p>Use these when the colour means something. They follow the colour-blind toggle.</p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Badge.Categorical" Title="Categorical hues">
        <p>
            For distinguishing categories that have no ranking &mdash; a type, a queue, a source. Reach
            for these only after the semantic set, and never to imply good or bad.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Badge.Severity" Title="Severity scale" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            For a scale whose steps <strong>rank</strong>: an incident priority P1&ndash;P5, a CVSS band,
            a triage tier. <strong>The label has to say the rank</strong> &mdash; &ldquo;P2&rdquo;,
            &ldquo;High&rdquo;, &ldquo;3 of 5&rdquo; &mdash; because a bare coloured pill is a colour,
            not a position; <a href="/progress">Progress</a> takes the same ramp through
            <code>.progress-bar--sev-N</code>.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Size and icon">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Badge.Sizes" Title="Three sizes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            A badge is a label, not a target, so its sizes are a <strong>type</strong> scale rather than
            the <a href="/concepts">control heights</a> &mdash; <code>.badge--lg</code> is 12px text, not
            a 36px pill.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Badge.WithAnIcon" Title="With an icon" />
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="In context">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Badge.InContext" Title="Card headers and table cells"
                Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>The two places badges actually live.</p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Badge.AiLabel" Title="AI label on a value" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; open each.</strong> <a href="/agent-status"><code>.badge-ai</code></a> as a
            <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> marks one value an agent filled in and opens a
            <a href="/popover"><code>.popover</code></a> through <code>popovertarget</code> that says which
            model, from what, how sure &mdash; <code>.confidence</code> &mdash; and what was there before.
            <code>.popover-actions</code> holds the way back, &ldquo;Revert to the original&rdquo;, at the
            panel's foot; reverting is the app's, which then drops the label.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>.badge-ai--icon</code> is the sparkle alone, a square the height of a word badge, for a
            field or a cell where a word would crowd the value. It has no words on screen, so
            <strong>its name is a <code>.visually-hidden</code> span</strong> and says that the value was
            generated. In an <code>.input-group</code> it sits inside the field's border after the value; in a
            cell or a line of text, after the value it marks.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>
